  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM EDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nCurrently another hot day with highs once again expected to top out \nin the upper 80's to lower 90's across the eastern Tennessee Valley. \nWhile there are a few showers out there as of early this afternoon \nthe coverage and intensity should both be lower than what we \nexperienced yesterday with weaker synoptic forcing and lower PWAT \nvalues across the region. Similar conditions expected tomorrow and \nTuesday with more hot days expected. We'll see more afternoon \nshowers and thunderstorms once again... Forecast soundings suggest \nmore instability on Monday and Tuesday leading to more vigorous \nafternoon convection. We could see a few severe thunderstorms with \ndamaging straight line winds possible in the strongest storms. With \nthe multiple days of storms and very little movement in the mean \nflow indicates the storms could sit over the same area for a while \nleading to isolated flooding.\n\nBy the middle of the week we will FINALLY see a large scale pattern \nchange and usher in the first inklings of fall. Ridge builds in over \nCanada and a trough sets up closer to home that will slowly meander \nit's way through the eastern half o the United States for the rest \nof the week and into the weekend. This will much cooler temperatures \nstarting Wednesday and by Thursday through the weekend we should see \nhighs topping out only in the 70's for every except southeast TN \nwhere they may squeak across the 80 degree mark. This pattern will \nalso be fairly dry with a more Canadian airmass moving in. \n\nLooks like we'll finally get to open the windows this weekend!",
